
## Frontend architecture — Feature-Sliced Design

`{{srcDir}}/` uses FSD v2.1 with the layers `_app`, `_pages`, `shared`
(`features`/`entities` are added when a second consumer appears). Read
[docs/fsd.md](docs/fsd.md) before adding a file under `{{srcDir}}/` — it has the
import boundary, the segment rules, and why `{{appDir}}/` only ever re-exports.

Rules that are easy to break without noticing:

- imports point downwards only, and a slice is imported through its public API (`index.ts`, or `index.server.ts` for server-only modules)
- `{{appDir}}/<route>/page.tsx` re-exports the FSD page **and its `metadata`** — nothing else (from `<slice>/index.server` when the page has a client leaf)
- `{{appDir}}/api/<name>/route.ts` re-exports the handler as the HTTP method (`export { getX as GET }` from `{{srcDir}}/_app/api-routes`) — nothing else; `middleware.js`/`instrumentation.js` stay at the project root
- `"use client"` goes on the leaf that needs it, not on the page
- `{{lintCommand}}` runs both linters: ESLint (`eslint.fsd.mjs`) flags a wrong-way or slice-internal import per file as you type, steiger (`steiger.config.ts`) flags the same imports plus what one file cannot show — a slice with no consumers, a layer sliced too finely
- editing `eslint.fsd.mjs`: flat config replaces a rule's options when a later block matches the same file, so all of a layer's `no-restricted-imports` patterns must stay in that layer's one block

Agents also load this as a skill at `.agents/skills/nextjs-fsd/SKILL.md` in
the repository root, symlinked from `.claude/skills/nextjs-fsd`, which carries
the same contract in the form they read first. `docs/fsd.md` is the source of
truth if the two disagree.

Scaffold with `nextjs-fsd generate page|slice|layout|api-route` rather than by hand, so
slices keep the same shape. Re-running one extends what exists (adds a
segment, a leaf, an error catalog) and never rewrites a file you already have.
`shadcn add <name>` is already aimed at `{{srcDir}}/shared/ui` by
`components.json`.
